/target

# makepkg works in place: it caches a bare clone of the source under the
# package's own name, checks it out into src/, stages the package under pkg/,
# and leaves the built tarballs beside the PKGBUILD. It also rewrites pkgver=
# in the PKGBUILD after running pkgver() — that one has to be reverted by hand,
# since the file itself is tracked.
#
# The built artifacts are unanchored, because makepkg leaves them wherever it
# is run and a stray run inside hoja-transfer/ once put 2.5GB of them past
# rules pinned to the repository root. Nothing else in the tree is named like
# this, so matching at any depth costs nothing.
*.pkg.tar.zst
*.pkg.tar.zst.sig
*.log

# The directories stay pinned, and must. makepkg's scratch space is called
# src/, and so is the application's — an unanchored rule here would ignore
# ./src and ./hoja-transfer/src, quietly untracking the whole program. Pinned
# rules catching only the sanctioned build location is the right trade: a
# stray run somewhere else leaves visible directories that `git status` will
# complain about, which is how it should be noticed.
packaging/hoja/
packaging/src/
packaging/pkg/
